 ## Operating characteristics
-**Time Complexity:** Dominated by sorting $N$ Gaussians, which is $O(N \log N)$ or $O(N)$ with radix sort [S3, unofficial]. Rasterization is $O(P \cdot K)$ where $P$ is pixels and $K$ is average Gaussians per pixel [S3, unofficial].
-**Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$ for Gaussian parameters and $O(P)$ for output buffers [S3, unofficial]. High VRAM usage: 4GB to view, 12GB to train [S3, unofficial]. Large disk size: 1GB+ for a scene [S3, unofficial].
-**Measured Performance:** Real-time rendering (60+ FPS) on modern GPUs for scenes with millions of Gaussians [S3, unofficial].
-**Scaling:** Scales linearly with number of Gaussians and pixels [S3, unofficial].
-**Strengths:** Real-time performance, high visual quality, differentiable [S3, unofficial].
-**Limitations:** High VRAM usage, large disk size, sorting bottleneck on non-CUDA hardware [S3, unofficial].
-**When to use:** Real-time rendering of learned radiance fields [S3, unofficial].
-**When not to use:** When hardware acceleration (CUDA) is unavailable and performance is critical, or when strict compatibility with existing triangle-based pipelines is required without adaptation [S3, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity**: Dominated by the sorting of $N$ Gaussians, typically $O(N \log N)$ or $O(N)$ with radix sort [S3, unofficial]. Rasterization itself is $O(P \cdot K)$ where $P$ is pixels and $K$ is average Gaussians per pixel.
+*   **Memory Complexity**: $O(N)$ for Gaussian storage. High VRAM usage: ~4GB to view, ~12GB to train [S3, unofficial]. Large disk size: 1GB+ for a scene [S3, unofficial].
+*   **Performance**: Real-time rendering is achievable on modern GPUs (e.g., RTX 3090/4090) at 1080p/4K [S3, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths**: Photorealistic quality, fast training, real-time rendering speed [S3, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations**: High VRAM/disk usage, static scenes (without 4D extensions), incompatibility with existing rendering pipelines (Vulkan, DirectX, WebGPU) without adaptation [S3, unofficial]. The primary bottleneck is sorting millions of Gaussians, which relies on CUB device radix sort (CUDA-specific) [S3, unofficial].
+*   **When to use**: Real-time visualization of learned radiance fields, VR/AR applications where quality and speed are critical.
+*   **When not to use**: Environments requiring strict compatibility with legacy graphics APIs, or where memory/disk constraints are severe.
 
 ## Background
-3D Gaussian Splatting was introduced in the paper "3D Gaussian Splatting for Real-Time Radiance Field Rendering" [S3, unofficial]. It allows real-time rendering of photorealistic scenes learned from small samples of images [S3, unofficial]. The method uses Structure from Motion (SfM) to estimate a point cloud, converts points to Gaussians, and trains them using Stochastic Gradient Descent [S3, unofficial]. The primary bottleneck is sorting millions of Gaussians, which is done efficiently in the original implementation using CUB device radix sort, a highly optimized sort only available in CUDA [S3, unofficial].
+3D Gaussian Splatting was introduced in the paper "3D Gaussian Splatting for Real-Time Radiance Field Rendering" by Kerbl et al. in 2023 [S3, unofficial]. It emerged as a rasterization-based alternative to NeRF, which relies on volumetric ray marching. The method represents scenes as 3D Gaussians, allowing for differentiable rendering and efficient training via stochastic gradient descent [S3, unofficial]. The original implementation was in CUDA, leveraging CUB for sorting [S3, unofficial]. Subsequent work has ported it to other platforms (e.g., MLX [S1, unofficial]) and extended it to dynamic scenes (4D GS) [S6, unofficial], panoramic cameras [S5], and various optimization techniques [S4, unofficial].
 
